<PageLayout
  title="Why melanocortins"
  kicker="Why this exists"
  description="One ancient gene keeps the whole body in balance — its colour, its hunger, its answer to stress, the way it heals. This page is the reason melanocortin.com exists: to map that system with equal parts wonder and rigour."
  updated="2026"
  about={[
    { name: 'Melanocortin', type: 'MedicalEntity', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melanocortin'] },
    { name: 'Pro-opiomelanocortin', type: 'MedicalEntity', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proopiomelanocortin'] },
    { name: 'Melanocortin 1 receptor', type: 'MedicalEntity', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melanocortin_1_receptor'] },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    Long before there were drugs, there was the signal. A single ancient gene —
    pro-opiomelanocortin, or POMC — is cut, tissue by tissue, into a family of
    short peptides the body uses to keep itself in balance: to set the colour
    that shields skin from light, to answer a hard day with cortisol, to say
    <em>enough</em> at the end of a meal, to let an inflamed tissue stand down and
    heal. Five receptors read that signal; two natural brakes oppose it; and from
    that small, economical toolkit comes a startling share of human
    physiology.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>
  <p>
    For most of a century this was biology's most elegant footnote — admired,
    mapped, and undruggable. Then, in the span of a few years, it became one of
    medicine's most productive frontiers. melanocortin.com exists to hold both of
    those truths at once: the wonder of a system the body already uses to protect
    and repair itself, and the discipline of saying only what the evidence will
    bear.
  </p>

  <h2>The visible tip</h2>
  <p>
    Pigmentation is the part of this system you can see, and it is the smallest
    part of what the system does. On the surface of every pigment cell sits MC1R,
    a switch with two settings. Turned up — by α-MSH, the system's own messenger
    — it lays down <strong>eumelanin</strong>, the brown-black pigment that
    absorbs ultraviolet light and shields the DNA beneath it. Left quiet, the same
    cell defaults to <strong>pheomelanin</strong>, a red-gold pigment that is a
    poor sunscreen and, under UV, can generate the very damage melanin is meant to
    prevent.<Cite n={2} /> The tan you build in the sun is not decoration; it is
    this switch doing its job — the melanocortin system reading the light and
    answering with protection.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>

  <PigmentSwitch />

  <p>
    For some people, the switch is quiet from birth. The common "red-hair" variants
    of <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a> never traffic properly to the cell
    surface, so even when the messenger arrives, less of the signal gets
    through.<Cite n={3} /> The visible result is fair skin, freckles, hair that
    runs to red or gold — and skin that burns where others tan. The invisible
    result is a lifetime of greater exposure to what sunlight does to unshielded
    DNA.<Cite n={2} /> It is, in the most literal sense, a difference written into
    one receptor.
  </p>

  <h2>What it asks of you</h2>
  <p>
    To live with that switch turned low is to learn a discipline early — the
    routine most people never think about and those who burn can never quite
    forget: sun protection made a habit, shade found on instinct, the quiet
    arithmetic of what a bright day will cost. Carried grudgingly, it curdles into
    self-consciousness. Carried with understanding — knowing exactly which cell,
    which receptor, which pigment is at stake — it becomes something closer to
    stewardship of your own skin. Knowing the mechanism doesn't lift the burden,
    but it changes the mindset you carry it with; and a burden you understand is
    one you can keep carrying, consistently, for a lifetime. Helping with that
    understanding is part of why this site exists.
  </p>

  <h2>Medicine learned to answer</h2>
  <p>
    Here is what turns wonder into more than a feeling: the switch can be spoken
    to. Afamelanotide is a sturdier copy of the body's own α-MSH — an MC1R agonist
    that drives protective eumelanin directly, without waiting for the sunlight
    that, for some people, only causes harm.<Cite n={4} /> In 2019 it became the
    first treatment ever approved for erythropoietic protoporphyria, a disorder in
    which light is not an inconvenience but agony; people who had organised their
    whole lives around avoiding the sun gained real, measured hours of
    tolerance.<Cite n={5} /> It is a small, exact proof of something larger — that
    a signal the body has always used to protect itself can be restored on
    purpose, and that the melanocortin pigment switch is genuinely druggable, even
    genuinely kind. See the full entries on{' '}
    <a href="/afamelanotide">afamelanotide</a> and{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R, the pigment switch</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is also where we are careful. The line between a medicine that restores
    protection for a painful disease and a peptide sold online to tan on demand is
    a real one, and we draw it in the open — risks and all — on the{' '}
    <a href="/afamelanotide">afamelanotide</a> and <a href="/melanotan">melanotan</a>{' '}
    pages. Reverence for the biology is not the same as a recommendation, and we
    never let the first quietly become the second.
  </p>

  <h2>The two truths</h2>
  <p>
    So we hold two truths on every page. We revere the biology — a system this
    old, this economical, this quietly central to being alive earns awe. And we
    cite the claims — because awe without rigour is just marketing, and the reader
    deserves better than to be sold to. Where the science is settled, we say so
    plainly. Where it is unsettled, we call it the frontier, not a sure thing. The
    wonder is real, and it is earned page by page. New here? Start with{' '}
    <a href="/system">the melanocortin system</a> — the whole map in one page — or
    read how we work in the <a href="/about">editorial standards</a>.
  </p>

